WHY THE TWO TIGHTENINGS ARE LOAD-BEARING
----------------------------------------
The fallback answers the same question as the FTS arm, so it must serve the same
ROW SET and mean the same thing by "present":

  * visibility — the FTS arm filters `(active = 1 OR compacted = 1) AND
    COALESCE(display_kind,'') <> 'hidden'`; the LIKE arm did not, so a hidden
    scaffolding row or a rewound row came back through the fallback exactly on
    the queries MATCH could not answer. Upstream applies
    `_search_filter_clauses` on every route including LIKE
    (hermes_state_search.py:163-190), and the stale arm in this file and the
    Turso backend already filter the same way.
  * literal presence — `%`, `_` and `\` are escaped (`ESCAPE '\'`), so a user
    term can no longer wildcard-match a row that does not contain it.
